Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	## Safety / regression evidence
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	- **Contracts and risk:** High-risk ownership/concurrency change: `durable-ack`, `recovery-generation`, `reader-snapshot`, `historical-input`, `query-meaning`. Preserve native admission, storage formats, isolation and configured durability. New handle and disposal exception contracts are documented in the [migration guide](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/blob/codex/transaction-handles/docs/transaction-handles.md).
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	- **Prior states and interactions:** Direct/Shared; legacy, ordinary and handle calls; live cursors and concurrent handles; pooled hosts; maintenance/close; encrypted and historical files; external writers; abandoned sessions; reused wrappers with fresh cores. Preserve inherited recovery, compatibility and fuzz coverage on the integrated stack.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	- **Failure outcomes:** Acknowledged commits survive under the stated fault model; known rollbacks leak no writes; uncertain commits remain indeterminate. Cancel blocked admission before close drains actual work. Fence new operations for maintenance while admitting existing dependencies. Cleanup must not repeat a peer's fatal error or hide its own failure. External writes must be visible after reacquisition and cold reopen.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	- **Evidence:** Eight real known-bad comparisons against `560529066` plus two inherited package proofs; permanent guards, registered fault hooks, explicit coverage dispositions and required platform evidence. [Audit and finite gate](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/blob/codex/transaction-handles/docs/transaction-handles-safety-review.md). **Candidate `eb01f346e` is undergoing full CI against dev**: [CI/Safety evidence](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36698371646), [Regression proof](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36698371173), [Fuzz](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36698371190), [compatibility](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/actions/runs/36698371399). Earlier green results are not substituted for this candidate. The prior Windows x86/net8 opaque pin-test timeout is retained in the audit; its phase and product cause remain unknown. Policy checks pass against dev.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	- **Coverage delta and residual concerns:** Three intentional test replacements and inherited CI/harness changes are accounted in the coverage ledger. No new quarantine or weakened assertions. The newly merged upstream close-checkpoint test now requires the exact surfaced IOException while retaining every WAL/recovery assertion; this intentional error-contract change is documented in the migration guide. Native-wait/pending-begin tests prove the admission path; Framework reuse, concurrent Direct handles and capability cases are added. Strongest counterexample: close/maintenance waiting on a continuation it prevents, including fatal shutdown and disposal races. Forced interleavings and independent review address it; tests cover exceptions/process death and inherited modeled persistence faults, not physical power removal. Existing quarantines and unrelated #3034 roadmap work remain explicit limits.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	### Current implementation and review fixes
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	`BeginTransaction()` returns an independent handle whose collections, queries and readers stay bound across sequential thread handoff. Ordinary calls remain independent. Overlapping public use and callback reentry reject before execution; completed handles release their session/application graph.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Sequential Shared handles reuse the **holder worker and child SharedEngine wrapper**. **The storage core still closes/reopens, and native writer ownership is released between every transaction.** No coherent engine/page cache survives an external writer. Cleanup also releases idle data/log handles, coordination participation and the child's mode-admission lease. Changed password/serialized collation invalidates the wrapper. The worker pool retains at most two idle threads for one second.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The supplied review enumerated eight production findings (despite its nine-defect summary). All eight have fixes and permanent guards:
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	1. Shared close cancels blocked native admission before draining the legacy owner.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	2. Pending maintenance blocks fresh readers while existing transaction/cursor dependencies finish.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	3. Raw engine close wakes collection-lock waiters before draining; it preserves maintenance service generations.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	4. Shared handle begin rejects caller-owned locking readers and pinned legacy ownership before scheduling.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	5. Disposed reader/enumerator access validates again after admission but before abort handling, preserving prior writes even when disposal races the call. Real reader and disposal errors still fail the transaction.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	6. Handle and child disposal coordinate with session close without masking an exception in a `using` block.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	7. Cleanup distinguishes published fatal state from completed teardown and suppresses only the published peer error; its own I/O/invalid-data failures still propagate.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	8. Writable ownership reclaims stale sort scratch without allowing read-only peers to delete active spills.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Fresh specialized review found the deferred-fatal variant of #7, a capture/disposal race in #5, and two evidence weaknesses; those were fixed and re-reviewed. The final child-admission review passed 36/36 cases on each net8/net10, adding eight forced plain/encrypted close-race cases. The new race guard fails on the actual previous head `3e88454e2`; twelve permanent variants distinguish rejected misuse from genuine failures. The final cleanup review passed 33 cases, including six independent variants. Its mutation control rejected broad error suppression. Earlier maintenance and Shared reviews also found no remaining in-scope defect. These are bounded reviews, not universal safety claims.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Final production source `3b60d5aea`: **220 handle/lifetime/abandonment/sort cases pass on each net8/net10**, with zero failures or skips. The independent child-admission review passed its 36-case selection on both runtimes. Counts overlap and are not summed. Relevant earlier integrated partitions passed 383 Shared and 200 native-admission cases per runtime. The maintenance/process-harness rerun passed 178 per runtime with one existing culture quarantine. Production Release builds pass all library targets. Hosted CLR4 and platform execution remains part of the current gate.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Checkpoint `2179ba651` passed all ten hosted regression proofs. Its platform runs exposed caller-owned test streams left open during cold reopen on macOS/Windows; fixed by closing the streams before verification. The earlier local process suite exposed obsolete reflection argument counts; fixed without removing process-death assertions. Failed attempts remain in the evidence archive. The superseded checkpoint CI was cancelled after the corrected candidate was pushed.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The full `1eaeaf092` suite additionally exposed the upstream close-checkpoint test's old expectation of a silently swallowed failure. `06c5b45ae` requires the exact original error and retains every recovery assertion; all eight checkpoint cases pass on net8/net10. No production change was needed. The superseded run and this semantic classification are retained.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The `06c5b45ae` Windows matrix caught a raw native-probe harness path containing a lexical `.` component, which is invalid after adding the Windows extended-path prefix. `52541f84d` probes the normalized filename, retaining both real alias-rejection checks and adding released-before/after controls. All 20 targeted cases pass on net8/net10; independent review found no weakened assertion. Candidate `3e88454e2` included this test-only correction. The later child-admission fix changes production code; its measurements were rerun on `3b60d5aea`. The superseded CI run was cancelled after the successor was pushed; its completed proof, fuzz and compatibility workflows passed.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The `3b60d5aea` native macOS Intel/net10 session exhausted its 300-second budget after 637 passing cases and one existing platform skip, with steady progress at the deadline; the same head completed all 642 cases on net8. Candidate `010821392` splits the exact original native selection into three checked, disjoint sessions, retaining the timeout and runtime/hook guards in each. Review also corrected case-sensitive discovery accounting to match VSTest’s case-insensitive selection: 21 lowercase-rebuild methods had executed but were absent from the old discovery record. These are CI/evidence defects, with the failed run retained. All 96 policy tests pass; no library code changes, dropped tests or unchanged rerun are involved. Both corrected macOS Intel jobs passed on this head. Its later Windows x86/net8 failure is separate: the legacy pin test gives child startup plus twenty independent commits one opaque 20-second wait. The log does not establish which phase stalled. Candidate `eb01f346e` separates child startup from actual write progress, preserves all twenty ordinary commits and keeps the pin owner alive. Only completed commits renew the 20-second progress deadline; a 60-second overall deadline remains. Plain/encrypted controls hold a real writer pin and require the probe to reject the blocked child. Exact cold records, payloads, indexed results and an unrelated sentinel are checked. All 15 focused cases pass on each net8/net10, and independent review found no remaining issue. The changed budget is explicitly recorded in the coverage ledger; no product-cause or green-CI claim is made.
